Myth: In the ancient days, there lived a potter named Thalia who shaped vessels from dream-clay. Each vessel was meant to hold a single pure emotion—one for grief, one for wonder, one for rage, one for love. But when filled, the emotions always spilled and mixed. Grief leaked into wonder, rage softened into love, creating muddy, confused feelings.

Thalia wept, smashing each contaminated vessel. "Why can emotions," she asked the kiln's fire, "never stay separate and clear?"

