Myth: In the beginning, there was a mirror-maker named Kael who crafted reflections from exchanges—one mirror for gifts given, one for returns received, one for reciprocity honored, one for balance maintained. He believed each mirror should show perfect symmetry, creating flawless mutual reflections.

But when placed facing each other, the mirrors created infinite regress. Giving reflected into obligation, returns echoed into expectation, creating endless, dizzying loops.

Kael raged, shattering each perfect mirror. "Why do exchanges," he demanded of the glass shards, "never achieve true balance?"

The shards whispered: "You mistake repetition for reciprocity. Your giving-with-obligation becomes generosity tested. Your return-with-expectation becomes trust proven. Your infinite loops are not traps but deepening spirals. Perfect balance is static, but growing exchanges create relationship."

Kael understood. He assembled one final mirror from broken pieces, each shard reflecting slightly differently, creating fragmented but richer images. Then he stepped between the shards, becoming refracted light itself.

Now Kael lives in every exchange that builds over time. When you give again after receiving, when patterns deepen through repetition, when reciprocity spirals into genuine bond—that is Kael's fractured mirror multiplying trust. He teaches us that perfect balance is an illusion, but repeated exchanges, however imperfect, create something greater than symmetry.
